1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,274
Can one X-ray angle hide a narrowed
artery?

2
00:00:03,715 --> 00:00:06,269
Look at this simulated angiogram.

3
00:00:06,711 --> 00:00:09,590
A nearby branch overlaps the area.

4
00:00:09,834 --> 00:00:13,688
When the view changes, the same narrowing
is easier to see.

5
00:00:14,292 --> 00:00:19,435
A coronary angiogram is a moving X-ray
study of the arteries that feed the heart

6
00:00:19,493 --> 00:00:24,752
muscle. Contrast enters through a catheter
and outlines the channel that blood

7
00:00:24,810 --> 00:00:30,174
can pass through. The team watches the
moving images to trace each artery and

8
00:00:30,232 --> 00:00:32,125
look for changes in the outline.

9
00:00:32,728 --> 00:00:37,419
The team identifies the main branches
before deciding where a narrowing is.

10
00:00:38,023 --> 00:00:42,504
In this example, the left main artery
divides into the left anterior descending

11
00:00:42,574 --> 00:00:44,617
artery and the circumflex.

12
00:00:45,314 --> 00:00:48,333
A separate view shows the right coronary
artery.

13
00:00:48,716 --> 00:00:53,639
Following the branches helps the team
describe the exact location of a finding.

14
00:00:54,150 --> 00:00:56,251
Here is one short narrowing.

15
00:00:56,495 --> 00:01:00,605
Contrast still passes through it and
reaches the artery beyond.

16
00:01:01,208 --> 00:01:05,980
The team compares the narrowest part with
a nearby reference segment and describes

17
00:01:06,026 --> 00:01:10,241
where the narrowing is, how long it is,
and how severe it looks.

18
00:01:10,845 --> 00:01:14,943
A narrowing and a complete blockage look
different on the images.

19
00:01:15,547 --> 00:01:19,680
The X-ray turns three-dimensional arteries
into a flat image.

20
00:01:20,191 --> 00:01:25,264
From one angle, branches can overlap or a
segment can look shorter than it is.

21
00:01:25,961 --> 00:01:30,442
A second view can separate the branches
and show more of the same narrowing.

22
00:01:31,139 --> 00:01:35,376
The clinician compares the recordings
before describing what they see.

23
00:01:36,073 --> 00:01:40,972
The size of a narrowing on the picture
gives the team one part of the answer.

24
00:01:41,669 --> 00:01:46,765
If its effect on blood supply is unclear,
the team may use a thin pressure-sensing

25
00:01:46,835 --> 00:01:51,642
wire. Tests called F F R or I F R

26
00:01:51,967 --> 00:01:55,577
compare pressure beyond the narrowing with
pressure upstream.

27
00:01:56,274 --> 00:01:58,723
The results help guide treatment
decisions.

28
00:01:59,165 --> 00:02:03,669
Sometimes the major arteries show no
important blockage, even though a person

29
00:02:03,727 --> 00:02:08,952
has symptoms. Small blood vessels can work
poorly, or a coronary

30
00:02:09,021 --> 00:02:11,193
artery can tighten temporarily.

31
00:02:11,889 --> 00:02:16,359
The care team uses the symptoms and other
findings to decide whether more tests

32
00:02:16,440 --> 00:02:18,461
could help explain what is happening.

33
00:02:19,064 --> 00:02:21,526
The result goes into a larger decision.

34
00:02:21,769 --> 00:02:26,761
The team considers the symptoms, urgency,
which arteries are involved and any other

35
00:02:26,819 --> 00:02:32,067
tests. Care may include medicines and
prevention, further assessment,

36
00:02:32,218 --> 00:02:37,152
a catheter procedure such as angioplasty
and stenting, or a discussion about

37
00:02:37,222 --> 00:02:42,005
bypass surgery. In a heart attack,
decisions may need to happen

38
00:02:42,098 --> 00:02:42,876
quickly.

39
00:02:43,387 --> 00:02:48,054
When the team explains an angiogram, three
questions can make the result easier to

40
00:02:48,135 --> 00:02:51,282
understand. Which artery was involved?

41
00:02:51,491 --> 00:02:54,904
Is there evidence that the narrowing
affects blood supply?

42
00:02:55,345 --> 00:02:58,329
What follow-up or treatment does the team
recommend?

43
00:02:58,933 --> 00:03:02,137
Those answers connect the picture to a
plan for care.
